{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And the field and the cave that was in it rose up.</b> But it already said above: ״ויקם שדה עפרון אשר במכפלה״ and so forth, ״to Avraham for a possession.״ But the first ״ויקם שדה עפרון״ was after payment and before the burial, to say that the field of Efron rose up to Avraham for a possession; and after the burial it mentions ״ויקם השדה,״ and it uses the language of אחוזה (holding), because when he buried his dead there, then he was established in the land and it was his holding. And it says ״מאת בני חת״ — from the sons of Heth — when it should have said ״מאת עפרון״ — from Efron; but the first mention came to say that it was from Efron that it came to Avraham for a possession, and therefore it mentions ״שדה עפרון״ — the field of Efron — and not ״השדה״ — the field; but here it says ״ויקם השדה״ to say that the field rose up to Avraham as a burial holding from the sons of Heth, who never contested it." "<b>And there are</b> in this parashah eight occurrences of בני חת, and one at the end of the section (Genesis 25:10): ״השדה אשר קנה אברהם מאת בני חת,״ and one at the end of the book, in the matter of Yaakov (Genesis 49:32): ״מקנה השדה והמערה אשר בו מאת בני חת״ — behold, ten." "<b>And it appears</b> that it is a great merit for the sons of Heth, who consented to the burial place, that they should be mentioned in the Torah ten times in the burial of all the patriarchs." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash: how many inkwells are spilled and how many pens are broken to write ten times in the Torah בני חת — corresponding to the Ten Commandments. And this teaches you that whoever clarifies the purchase of a righteous person is as if he fulfills the Ten Commandments. And so we find in Divrei HaYamim, when David bought the threshing floor from Aravnah the Jebusite, ten times [the name] Arnan appears. And one should contemplate this parashah, which hints that even if a person's greatness grows and the whole world and its fullness were his, he has nothing in it but his grave of four cubits. For Avraham — the entire land was given to him as a gift, and the first thing he acquired there was the cave of Makhpelah, which was in Kiryat Arba, which is Hevron. And even though Israel, upon their entry into the land, took most of it by the sword, we find three places that were superior to all other places, which came into their hands by purchase with money, and they are: Hevron, Mount Gerizim, Mount Eival, and Mount Moriah. Hevron, as it is written here: ״לאברהם למקנה,״ and it is written: ״השדה אשר קנה אברהם״ and so forth. Mount Gerizim and Mount Eival, where we made a covenant and accepted the Torah upon ourselves with oaths, and it is written concerning it (Genesis 33:19): ״ויקן את חלקת השדה.״ Mount Moriah, which is what is written (II Samuel 24:24): ״ויקן דוד את הגרן״ and so forth."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {3 "Hebrew text explicitly enumerates three places (חברון, הר גריזים והר עיבל) but then adds הר המוריה as a fourth; translated faithfully without reconciling the apparent discrepancy. The prooftext for Gerizim and Eival is Genesis 33:19 (Shekhem), which rabbinic tradition identifies with that region."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:35.118159548Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}